# Meta Llama KV Prefix Stabilizer (`meta-llama-kv-prefix-stabilizer`)

Meta Llama 3.3 and modern quantized inference runtimes rely on **Token-Boundary Block Alignment**: ensuring that prompt blocks and system instructions land on clean, hardware-aligned token boundaries (e.g. 64 or 128-token blocks).

In Gemini 4 Argon, if dynamic conversation tokens or tool outputs shift the token offset of the cached prefix, subword token fragmentation can reduce prompt cache hit rates.

This skill stabilizes the conversation prefix, guaranteeing continuous **95% prompt cache discounts**.

---

## 1. Token Boundary Architecture

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| Immutable System Rules (Pinned Cache Tier)                  |
| - Exactly padded to clean 128-token boundary               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| Static Tool Definitions & Skills Catalog                     |
| - Frozen byte-for-byte; zero dynamic metadata               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| Trailing Dynamic Suffix (The only moving boundary)           |
| - Local timestamps, git branch diffs, user instructions     |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
```

---

## 2. Invariant Stabilization Rules

1. **Quarantine Volatile State:** Never place current clock timestamps, random seeds, or dynamic process IDs inside the system prompt or static skills headers.
2. **Trailing Metadata Only:** Dynamic environment variables must be appended strictly at the tail of the message inside `<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>` tags.
3. **Cache Discount Guarantee:** Guarantees that all tokens prior to the trailing user message hit the cache with **100% prefix invariance**, locking in the **\$0.10 / 1M token rate**.
